A}^{T}\\,Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 6 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 6 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}6 & -18 \\\\ -18 & 54\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ 30\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the final matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & -18 & -10 \\\\ -18 & 54 & 30\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}6 & -18 & -10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-\\frac{5}{3}+3{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{5}{3}+3{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{5}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n12a+\\frac{b}{c}=12\\cdot (-\\frac{5}{3})+\\frac{3}{1}=-17.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415140 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415137 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415140 #edb/ref 1069 "q-136945" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415140 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415140 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $b$ shown above. If the general least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}c \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+3b+c?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415140 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "090045f3-969e-5b87-b1e6-6f48922501d3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415140 #edb/ref 1074 "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}6 & -6 & -4 \\\\ -6 & 6 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 4 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the above matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & -6 & -4 & 2 \\\\ -6 & 6 & 4 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 4 & 4 & -4\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}↔{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 4 & 4 & -4 \\\\ -6 & 6 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 6 & -6 & -4 & 2\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -6 & 6 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 6 & -6 & -4 & 2\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-6){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+6{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & -4\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns (the $1$st and $3$rd). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{3}=-2,\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n-{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+(-2)=-1\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-1+{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1+{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+3b+c=0+3\\cdot (-2)+1=-5.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415141 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415135 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415141 #edb/ref 1069 "q-137092" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415141 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415141 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=1 \\\\ 4{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=1 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear equations shown above. If the general least-squares solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $6a+\\frac{4b}{c}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415141 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4a92698e-17e4-57b8-8191-7aa9adc0e63f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415141 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we write our system in matrix form as $Ax=b,$ where \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}24 & -12 \\\\ -12 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the final matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}24 & -12 & 4 \\\\ -12 & 6 & -2\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}24 & -12 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n6a+\\frac{4b}{c}=6\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}}{1}=3.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415144 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186415145 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415144 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "40c07ba2-e36f-5f6c-9e19-b8d5be6314d0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415144 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186415139 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415145 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186415148 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415145 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b7f52acc-285e-5a90-8147-1ce492061ed5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415145 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186415141 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415146 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186415144 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415146 #ed